    "title": "Texas skater rolls onto big screen in ‘One Battle After Another’",
    "text": "El Paso native Gilberto Martinez (second from left) and director Paul Thomas Anderson on the set of One Battle After Another. Courtesy of Gilbert MartinezSkateboarding has always been a big part of Gilberto Martinez Jr.’s life. Born and raised in El Paso, Martinez started skateboarding when he was 7 years old and was on the frontlines when it made a resurgence in the late 1990s and early 2000s.“It’s something I've been doing for more than half of my life,” Martinez, 34, told MySA during a recent interview. “We didn’t have any skate parks growing up, so my skate parks were the streets downtown.”Article continues below this adMartinez said his life as a skateboarder came full circle when he and three other El Paso skateboarders – Julian Corral, Elijah Sambrano and Luis Trejo – were cast in the Oscar-nominated film One Battle After Another by writer-director Paul Thomas Anderson (There Will Be Blood).One Battle After Another follows a group of revolutionaries being tracked by the U.S. military for their past crimes. One of the radicals, Bob Ferguson (Leonardo DiCaprio), seeks help from his friend Sergio St. Carlos (Benicio del Toro) when his daughter Willa (Chase Infiniti) becomes embroiled in the pursuit.Gilberto Martinez on the set of One Battle After Another with stars Benicio del Toro and Leonardio DiCaprio.Courtesy of Gilbert MartinezMartinez plays BeeGee, the leader of a group of “vato skateboarders,” who attempt to help Sergio get Bob to a safe place after Col. Steven J. Lockjaw (Sean Penn) sends the military into a sanctuary city under the guise of immigration raids to kill Bob.Article continues below this adMartinez makes his big screen debut as BeeGee during a riot scene when he grabs onto the side of Sergio’s Thunderbird on his skateboard – a move known as “skitching” in skateboarding terms – and yells out, “It’s f---ing World War III out there!”“I'm skating in the movie in the streets that I grew up skating, which is kind of surreal,” Martinez said.Later, BeeGee and his buddies jump from rooftop to rooftop trying to get Bob out of harm’s way. Bob, however, takes a tumble and is arrested by police. BeeGee later calls Sergio to tell him that his friend had been captured.Article continues below this adMartinez said once Anderson and casting director Cassandra Kulukundis saw what he and his fellow El Paso skateboarders could do, the production team started “cooking up” more ideas for them.“They were like, ‘These guys are good!’” Martinez said. “‘We need to keep using them because they’ve got parkour skills. They can jump and run and they’re agile.’ When we’re running on the roof, we're doing ninja kicks and trying to jump higher than normal.”The skateboarding scenes were shot in El Paso over 11 days. Martinez, who works as a store manager at the skateboarding retail chain Zumiez and as a freelance graphic designer, said working with DiCaprio, del Toro and Anderson was a mind-blowing experience.“It gives me the chills just thinking about it now,” Martinez said. “There was so much camaraderie on the set. It was so fun being around them and their charisma. Everyone was in their zone.”Article continues below this adMartinez with Benicio del Toro and director Paul Thomas AndersonCourtesy of Gilbert MartinezAlong with his brush with Hollywood, Martinez is thrilled that One Battle After Another offered him a way to share his passion for skateboarding – and his love for his hometown – with the world.“Skateboarding has really given my life purpose and direction,” Martinez said. “It’s what helped lead me out of where I was trying to escape. And being cast in this film – it was a godsend. Me lo mandó Diosito.”March 15, 2026Kiko Martinez was born and raised in San Antonio. He earned his B.A. in Communications from Our Lady of the Lake University in 2003 and has written about film, entertainment, sports and culture for several outlets across the U.S. Kiko is a member of the Critics Choice Association and a founding member of the Latino Entertainment Journalists Association. He is a Rotten Tomatoes-certified film critic.",
